      {
        "id": "https://openalex.org/W7172427855",
        "doi": "10.1016/j.engstruct.2026.123504",
        "title": "Experimental study and numerical simulation on seismic performance of a self-centering deformation-amplified torsional steel tube damper",
        "published": "2026-08-04",
        "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.engstruct.2026.123504",
        "authors": [
          "De-bin Wang",
          "De-bin Wang",
          "Yuan-Ke Zhu",
          "Wenming Wang"
        ],
        "institutions": [
          "Dalian Jiaotong University",
          "Shandong Jianzhu University"
        ],
        "journal": "Engineering Structures",
        "abstract": "To address the limitations of conventional metallic dampers, which exhibit difficulty in yielding under small deformations and significant residual deformation under large deformations, this paper proposes a novel self-centering deformation-amplified torsional steel tube damper (SC-DATSTD). The structural configuration, working mechanism, and assembly process of the proposed damper are described in detail. Quasi-static cyclic loading tests were conducted to investigate its hysteretic behavior, with a focus on key performance indicators, including failure mode, load-bearing characteristics, energy dissipation efficiency, and self-centering capability. Furthermore, a refined finite element model was developed. Experimental results indicate that the hysteresis curves exhibit a distinct asymmetric flag-shaped pattern. Reducing the initial amplification angle effectively enhances both the deformation and load-bearing amplification factors of the energy-dissipation system, significantly improves its energy-dissipation performance under low displacement levels, and increases the equivalent damping ratio by up to 43.3%, while maintaining stable self-centering capacity. Numerical simulation results show good agreement with experimental data, with maximum load errors across all loading stages remaining within 10% and nearly identical tension-compression load ratios. The reduction in the initial amplification angle leads to markedly increased plastic deformation in the energy-dissipation steel tubes, consistent with experimentally observed energy dissipation trends, thereby confirming that the finite element model accurately captures the working mechanism and hysteretic behavior of the damper.",
        "citation_count": 0,
        "method": "Hybrid"
